THOUGHT: NullAway complains because existingLease is @Nullable yet dereferenced in the logging and return statements without a direct null-check. Even though those lines are control-flow guarded, NullAway is path-insensitive here. A minimal fix is to reuse the already-computed existingStatus and null-check existingLease before dereferencing, so behavior stays the same and only this site is changed. I’ll edit the file accordingly, then run the prescribed Gradle command to verify the error is gone.

```bash
cd /home/joos/projects/nullrepair/nullness-benchmarks/eureka && sed -i '35,40c\        if (existingLease != null) {
          logger.debug(
              "There is already an existing lease with status {}  for instance {}",
              existingStatus.name(),
              existingLease.getHolder().getId());
          return StatusOverrideResult.matchingStatus(existingStatus);
        }' eureka-core/src/main/java/com/netflix/eureka/registry/rule/LeaseExistsRule.java && nl -ba eureka-core/src/main/java/com/netflix/eureka/registry/rule/LeaseExistsRule.java | sed -n '20,60p' && ./gradlew eureka-core:compileJava -x test -q > build_output.log 2>&1 || grep -A 5 -B 5 'LeaseExistsRule.java' build_output.log
```

